\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The four segmentation error types on one cohort tree (left coronary tree of scan 14, front view, line width proportional to radius). (a) The tree with an inserted 80\% DS lesion in the proximal LAD (blue) and the measurement point 20~mm beyond it. (b) T1 deletes the largest side branch beyond the lesion (red). (c) T2 ends the host vessel 25~mm beyond the lesion, and the red part is lost. (d) Radius along the LAD for the caliber errors, T3 lengthening the lesion by 2.46~mm and T4 shrinking the radius by 7\% from the lesion onwards. The same instance was solved in 3D.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:design}{{1}{3}{The four segmentation error types on one cohort tree (left coronary tree of scan 14, front view, line width proportional to radius). (a) The tree with an inserted 80\% DS lesion in the proximal LAD (blue) and the measurement point 20~mm beyond it. (b) T1 deletes the largest side branch beyond the lesion (red). (c) T2 ends the host vessel 25~mm beyond the lesion, and the red part is lost. (d) Radius along the LAD for the caliber errors, T3 lengthening the lesion by 2.46~mm and T4 shrinking the radius by 7\% from the lesion onwards. The same instance was solved in 3D}{figure.1}{}}
